{% comment %}
    An identifier you can click to copy. Confirmation is the chip's own `copied`
    state (tw-inline-copy) rather than a toast — these sit in page headers and
    identifier tables, where a toast per click is far too loud.

    A <button> so it is keyboard-reachable and announces itself as an action.
{% endcomment %}
<button type="button"
        class="tw-inline-copy{% if class %} {{ class }}{% endif %}"
        :class="{ copied: copied }"
        x-data="copyableValue({ value: '{{ value|default:''|escapejs }}', hideValue: {{ hide_value|default:''|yesno:"true,false" }}, copyFrom: '{{ copy_from|default:""|escapejs }}', title: '{{ title|default:''|escapejs }}' })"
        @click="copyToClipboard()"
        :title="copied ? 'Copied' : (title || 'Copy to clipboard')"
        :aria-label="copied ? 'Copied' : (title || 'Copy to clipboard')">
    {% comment %}The value is server-rendered as well as bound: Alpine overwrites it with the same text on init, but without it the value reaches the page only as a JS string, so a public page ships an identifier no crawler or reader can see.{% endcomment %}
    {% comment %}break-all so a long unbroken identifier (a PURL or a full CPE) wraps inside the card instead of running off the edge on a narrow screen. The public identifier table relied on it before this component replaced the code block.{% endcomment %}
    <span x-show="!hideValue && value"
          x-text="value"
          x-cloak
          class="min-w-0 break-all">
        {% if not hide_value %}{{ value|default:'' }}{% endif %}
    </span>
    <i class="fas tw-inline-copy-icon"
       :class="copied ? 'fa-check' : 'fa-copy'"
       aria-hidden="true"></i>
    {# Announce the result to screen readers, which cannot see the colour change. #}
    <span class="sr-only"
          role="status"
          aria-live="polite"
          x-text="copied ? 'Copied to clipboard' : ''"></span>
</button>
